1. Introduction

This software is an improved version of KLIC (Ver 2.002) with
optimized compiler and runtime system for efficient goal scheduling.

2. Feature of the Software

Since the original version of KLIC dynamically schedules the execution
order of goals, its scheduling overhead may cause significant
performance degradation if your program consists of, for example,
communicating sequential processes.  This optimized version aims to
reduce the scheduling overhead by compile-time dependency analysis and
run-time scheduling exploiting the analysis result.

The compiler statically analyzes the dependency among goals to find
threads.  Since a thread consists of goals whose order of execution is
statically determined, the runtime system efficiently executes the
thread without scheduling overhead.  Moreover, this new scheduling
mechanism will drastically reduces GC invocation because it maintains
the execution environment of a thread using a stack.

Note that the current version of this software is a prototype and thus
it does NOT support the all features of the original KLIC.

3. Usage

The following instructions are the differences from the original KLIC.
See the documents of KLIC for common instructions not shown here.

3.1  Installation

Do "Configure" in the top directory, and then do "make."

Follow the following special instructions on "Configure."

  + The current version is only applicable to single processor single
    processor environment.  Thus reply "no" to the following query.

    Configure also for parallel implementations?  (yes or no) [no] no
                                                                   ~~
  + Optimization level of C compiler should be "none", that is;

    Which optimization level to use? [none] none
                                            ~~~~

3.2  Compilation

In directories sched-test/test0*/, "Makefile" for test00.kl1, ...,
test04.kl1 are available.  Each "Makefile" will makes the optimized
object (test0*) and also the ordinary object (test0*_normal) for each
test program.

3.3  Execution

The run-time option "-n" should always be specified.

The following options are available:

  + -sst <n> : stack size for each thread (default = 100 word)
  + -nst <n> : number of stacks for threads (default = 10)

Default values can be changed by modifying the values of STACKSIZE and
STACKNUM in the file "include/klic/sched.h".


4. Restrictions

This version is a prototype of the improved KLIC for evaluation.  Thus
it has the following restrictions.

4.1  Restrictions on Programming

  + Pragmas cannot be used, and thus parallel codes cannot be obtained.
  + Only basic data types (atom, integer, functor, list) are implemented.
  + Only the following basic built-in predicates are implemented.
    (a) =/2, wait/1, atomic/1, atom/1, integer/1, :=/2
    (b) arithmetics such as add/3, and macros such as '+' and '-'.
    (c) comparisons such as equal/2, and macros such as '=:='.

4.2  Restrictions on Compilation and Execution

  + Separated compilation is not available.
  + Runtime tracing is not available.
